/***
2. List : Doubly linked list:

Headerfile : 
<list>

Same as vector:
(i) Declaration 
(ii) Inserting : [push_back]
(iii) Deletion : [pop_back]
(iv) Size , empty
(v) Traverse : using auto

-----------------------------------------------------------
----------------------------------------------------------
Difference:
-> No random access : like v.begin() + 1 cannot do
--> Use iterator.
eg:
    Method - 1: Advance :
        eg: it = v.begin ();
            advance(it,2)
            // so changes original iterator : moves 2 step
    ----------------------------------------------------
    Method - 2: Next: 
                // returns new iterator , does not change
                original one
        eg: it = v.begin();
            it_new = next (it,2)
            *(it_new) // gives value
---------------------------------------------------------
Insert :

(i) vec_name. push_front (val) deletes first element
(ii) vec_name.push_back(val) deletes last element
(iii)insert ( it,val)
        it = v.begin()
        advance(it,1)
        insert(it,20)
---------------------------------------------------------
---------------------------------------------------------
Deletion:

(i)Pop_front : vec_name.pop_front()
(ii) Pop_back : v.pop_back()
(iii)erase (it)
        eg: it --> advance or next
----------------------------------------------------------
----------------------------------------------------------
Accessing: 
--> Using iterator:
--> advance (it,1)
*it --> value
--> * (next (it,1)) value
--> cannot access using index
--------------------------------------------------------
----------------------------------------------------------
Updation:
--> using iterator
--> *it = 20
----------------------------------------------------------
---------------------------------------------------------
Traversing:
for (auto i = v.begin();i!=v.end();i++)
{
cout<<*i;
----------------------------------------------------------
---------------------------------------------------------
}

